# Frozen preparation → publication write ledger — 22 October 2026

**Decision from this bounded source pass:** the frozen source exposes repeated representation of some render fields, but no demonstrated whole-callback deletion that justifies a hybrid implementation or predicts closure of the roughly 3 ms Node 1,024 median gap. Keep the correct `dec7509b3a18b2d71e27de023419f45c32fb10e7` variant held for parity admission. This is a source/retained-receipt ledger, not a timing run or a proof that parity is impossible. It neither selects a product patch nor changes the accepted synchronous-query contract. V3 remains preserved at `aac036f9`.

## Count domains and exact operation

The maintained Node `publish 1024 retained Text instances` callback sets all 1,024 `Text.text` values to `bravo NNNN` (or back to `alpha NNNN`), calls `scene.updateMatrixWorld(true)` once, checks group error, and returns text count; `commitState` checks follow the timed callback. The browser 1,024 callback sets the same ten UTF-16-unit labels, then makes one ordinary `renderer.render` call; measurement/readback checks occur outside timing. Node uses font size 16, exact width 160, default wrapping and capacity 16,384; browser uses font size 7, exact width 44, no wrap and capacity 2,048. These are different operations despite common text and font family. Sources: `benches/labs/package/stress.bench.ts:123–140`, `fixture.ts:238–260`, `benches/scripts/support/dense-browser-entry.js:70–110,223–243,321`.

| Count | What is established for the actual paths |
| --- | --- |
| 1,024 assignments; 10 UTF-16 units each | Exact in both fixture producers: 10,240 newly authored code units, 20,480 UTF-16 payload bytes before allocation/capacity effects. The setter path invokes one synchronous paragraph preparation per changed owner. |
| Prepared semantic glyphs | The browser readback reports `glyphCount: 10` for its first and last labels; it does not count all 1,024 prepared rows. The Node timed callback makes no such read. Total prepared semantic rows in either timed operation are **unknown from these receipts**. |
| Prepared `LayoutGlyph` source rows | **Unknown for the timed Node and browser paths.** A separate native Bitmap replay with a source-matched ten-unit producer observed 10,240 source rows, 9,216 selected rows and 1,024 recordless rows per direction. Its source count is a cross-check, not a transferable measurement of these timed callbacks. |
| Selected rendered instances | Browser 1,024 cold/warm readback records 9,216 instances; the matched Node profile reports one draw and 9,216 rendered glyphs. These are selected output counts, not evidence of 9,216 prepared `LayoutGlyph` or semantic rows. Sources: `benches/reports/held-single-owner-local-2026-10-22/evidence/dense-browser-candidate.json.raw` `oracles[1024]`, and `benches/reports/held-matched-profiles-local-2026-10-21/profile-differential-diagnosis-sol-high.md:11`. |
| Lines, placement segments, masks actually changed, Codec fields and bytes | The source determines their element types and conditional writes, but these exact operation counts are not in the timed receipts. Do not multiply by string length or selected-instance count. |

The separate native replay's exact gathered-field observation (97,280 f32 + 20,480 u32 slots, 589,824 physical bytes compared, 368,640 emitted) describes its own E4 path and is **not** a prepared-state byte count or an elapsed-time estimate. Its E3/E5 variants substituted fresh field gathering or direct accessor/candidate/Codec work for retained work; their sampled profiles did not demonstrate a complete-callback win. Sources: `held-gather-input-plan-local-2026-10-20/input-plan-high-review.md:15`, `held-direct-operand-local-2026-10-21/reviews/architecture-speed-zoomout-sol-high.md:21`, and `held-matched-profiles-local-2026-10-21/profile-differential-diagnosis-sol-high.md:59`.

## Producer, first consumer, and second write

| Prepared producer/output | First possible consumer; actual timed path | Later write/copy and why it exists |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ParagraphState::prepare_text`, Unicode, bidi, shaping runs, shape, clusters, geometry and `flow_layout` (`state.rs:4136–4255`) | Authoritative text identity, line flow, and subsequent positioning; immediate `measure()`, `glyphs()`, caret/selection and `split()` can demand this revision before render. The timed fixtures request no semantic sidecar. | Accepted candidates commit into retained state. The full-replacement preflight is adopted rather than reshaped, and the old-text seed was removed by this wave. `commit_text` maintains a reusable pending mirror; the old main path also did so. No second shaping or intermediate wire-buffer copy is present. |
| `PositionedSemanticGlyph` (56 bytes, asserted in `positioning.rs:109–140`), semantic line starts/counts (`u32`) and inline extents (`f64`), placement translations | `append_paragraph_measurement` → `layout_query::append_measurement_with_glyph_spans` reads these on requested measure/inspection (`state.rs:2392–2532`). Three's caret, selection, anchor and independent split reach prepared placements (`three/text.ts:1018–1159`). No timed query in either 1,024 callback; the contract still requires these values ready at setter return. | Publication also reads semantic glyph indices/translations for placement and gathers; independent `split()` copies selected semantic glyphs and placement rows into its own compiler (`state.rs:1114–1281`). Moving all positioning to render would make immediate queries prepare it on demand and change failure/revision timing. |
| `LayoutGlyph` (16 four-byte fields = 64 payload bytes) plus six f32 and six u32 base SoA columns (48 bytes per render-source row), optional three f32/two u32 effect columns (20 bytes), and one u16 change mask (2 bytes) | Built by `PositionedGlyphArena::build` / `push_glyph` during setter preparation (`positioning.rs:143–181,539–615,3084–3140`); semantic glyphs are pushed even when `local.has_outline` is false, whereas `LayoutGlyph` is conditional (`:2350–2420,:2960–3085`). Revision assignment first reads the columns against the prior prepared row and writes the mask at setter time; publication gather is their first downstream consumer in the timed paths (`positioning.rs:3140–3408`; `state.rs:2619–2701`). | Revision assignment compares current and prior fields, then writes `content_revision`, inherited `placement_slot` and accumulated unpublished mask (`positioning.rs:3140–3408`). Gather selects/reads source rows, writes its own `PlanGlyph`, source correspondence, packed field lanes and masks before Codec/ordered/session physical plan (`codec_gather.rs:1465–1530,1750–1895`; `state.rs:1865–2007`). These are a later representation for renderer planning, not a second shape. `copy_glyphs` independently clones selected `LayoutGlyph`, semantic and SoA rows on demand (`state.rs:1150–1269`). |
| Decoration records, line decoration spans, visual/bidi line metadata | Decoration rows are consumed by publication gather and `copy_decorations` (`state.rs:1284–1353,2646–2700`); line/visual data support prepared inspection and positioning. | The two fixtures author no decoration/effect style, so no decoration output or optional effect columns are established for them. Decoration counts in an arbitrary styled operation remain variable; do not erase the general path. |
| Session placements, selected `PlanGlyph`, physical Codec buffers, ordered batches, renderer-owned bytes | First built/consumed at publication; `copy_glyphs`/`copy_decorations` build separate independent plans when requested. | Retained gather/plan state and in-flight renderer bytes have distinct lifetimes. Publication may compare old/new physical data, emit patches or rebuild a checkpoint. The exact timed-path field/patch byte counts remain unknown; native replay numbers above do not fill that gap. |

The payload formula for a newly populated render-source row is **64 + 48 + 2 = 114 bytes**, excluding semantic glyphs, optional effects, line/placement records, allocator capacity, revision rewrites and later gather/physical copies. For the separate native replay's 10,240 source rows this formula is 1,167,360 payload bytes; it is **neither measured memory traffic nor an actual timed Node/browser multiplier**. The `PositionedSemanticGlyph` contributes 56 bytes per semantic row, whose actual timed count is unknown. The 9,216 selected browser/Node output count alone cannot price the setter's source-row writes.

There is a concrete overlapping representation within those source rows: `push_glyph` writes `font_size`, `raster_pixel_ratio`, `inline_extent`, `block_extent` into both `LayoutGlyph` and four base f32 columns, and `stable_id` into both `LayoutGlyph` and one base u32 column. That is 20 nominal payload bytes per render-source row before any compiler optimization, capacity or extra derivation. It is a possible **local representation deletion**, not an established 20-byte traffic saving or a ready hybrid: the Codec accepts contiguous SoA fields, source revision diff consumes them, and independent split copies them. Deriving the values at gather must preserve field order, source errors and exact bits and may add accessor/candidate work. E3/E5 already expose that substitution risk; do not infer a latency gain from duplicate storage alone.

## Reverse row-layout alternative: keep SoA, narrow `LayoutGlyph`

The opposite direction is source-coherent in a narrow form: retain all six base f32 and six base u32 contiguous columns, the current packed gather/Codec loops, and `LayoutGlyph.stable_id`; remove only the four f32 values duplicated in `semantic_f32[2..6]` from retained `LayoutGlyph`: inline/block extent, font size and raster pixel ratio. The record would be 48 rather than 64 payload bytes per render-source row. The producer can write those values once into the existing SoA from its current style/local glyph inputs; no new semantic store, second packed layout or full `LayoutGlyph` candidate is needed. In the **separate native replay** with 10,240 source rows, the nominal AoS payload reduction is 163,840 bytes per newly built candidate. The corresponding timed Node/browser source-row count and actual writes are unmeasured, so this is an operation count, not a traffic or latency saving. The duplicate stable ID is another four bytes, but it is the hot retained-source identity and split-selection key; removing it would spread a co-indexed lookup through identity/reorder/gather paths and is excluded from this bounded variant.

This is not a free deletion of the revision pass. `PositionedGlyphArena::semantic_change_mask` already compares the six SoA f32 fields, including these four, against the prior preparation (`positioning.rs:3361–3408`); removing AoS copies deletes no such comparison or content-revision/mask write. It can remove four AoS producer field writes and 16 bytes per source row from a *copy that actually happens*, such as `append_retained_line`/`retain_static_glyph_state` (`positioning.rs:1040–1189`) or an independently requested split (`state.rs:1150–1200`). Whether either retained copy runs on the exact dense alpha→bravo callbacks is unknown; do not count it as a dense saving. Semantic and SoA writes, change-mask accumulation, shape/flow/position, selected `PlanGlyph` creation, Codec physical bytes, and the 1,024 setter transactions remain.

The replacement reads are precise. Both retained `update_retained_source` and fresh `append_from` call `binding.select(glyph_id, font_size, raster_pixel_ratio)` for each visited source row (`codec_gather.rs:896,1517`); the two values would become indexed reads of SoA columns 4 and 5. `plan_glyph` needs columns 2 and 3 for selected `PlanGlyph` bounds (`codec_gather.rs:2081–2110`), and `update_retained_position_only` checks those bounds before reusing a record (`:1003–1040`). Publication's reorder preparation also calls `binding.select` from retained glyphs (`state.rs:1733–1752`). `push_glyph` currently copies all four floats out of its AoS argument to fill SoA (`positioning.rs:3080–3118`); the producer would instead pass the four already computed values to those columns. Independent split already copies both the selected AoS row and all SoA columns, so a narrower AoS row preserves its own complete copy with the same indices; its compiler still needs the same selected plan and placement rows.

This minimizes the E3/E5 risk of replacing contiguous Codec operands with per-load accessors: `gather_fields`, `update_fields`, packed SIMD inputs and physical buffer emission can stay as they are. It **does** move two reads into every source-row selection and two bounds reads into selected plan/reuse paths, possibly changing cache locality and error precedence when an internal `LayoutPlanInput` has missing/misaligned fields. A coherent implementation would prove the package-owned producer keeps column lengths aligned, preserve exact bits and first failure on all supported paths, with an explicit compatibility decision for malformed internal inputs; a second executor is not justified. The public JavaScript API/ABI need not change, but the Rust `LayoutGlyph`/`LayoutPlanInput` module is exported by the shaper crate (`lib.rs:8`, `engine/mod.rs:37`), so its source-level users and compatibility must be audited before removing fields. Under the engine call contract, this must not add a persistent failure or a user-visible error route.

**Assessment:** plausible local representation simplification, not yet an admitted parity wave. It names at most 16 nominal AoS bytes per prepared source row and no removed revision comparisons, while introducing indexed gather reads. It cannot be priced against the roughly 3 ms Node deficit from this ledger. The next decisive evidence is exact-fixture counts for new/copy paths and a narrow source/attribution proof of *actual* AoS writes avoided versus added SoA reads, followed by complete-operation and correctness gates only if that deletion remains material. Do not combine this with a render-boundary move or another gather algorithm in one candidate.

## Boundary and admission implication

The present candidate already avoids the obvious full-replacement old-text seed, repeated single-owner routing, empty JS frame sections and second eleven-stage commit for settled owners (`held-single-owner-local-2026-10-22/README.md`). Remaining per-set validation/admission is real lost batching: `measure_paragraph_inner` validates codec/revisions/lifecycle, preflights the owner, prepares it, and commits before returning, 1,024 times. Main instead records setters and performs shared preparation in one publication transaction. That setup is not made free by deferring render projection; any hybrid still has 1,024 authoritative setter commits and must render the 9,216 selected instances. In the browser matched fixture the candidate's setter/publication p50 split is 9.0/4.0 ms versus main 1.3/11.3 ms at WebGPU 1,024, while whole p50 differs by 0.4 ms; these marginal phase medians are not additive causal savings. The Node maintained operation remains 19.548 ms candidate versus 16.547/16.686 ms controls, with a distinct fixture. The single slow Node 512 block is retained evidence, not a tail-only rejection.

A bounded hybrid would have to keep the semantic row and line/translation state authoritative synchronously, defer **only** a proven render projection, and use the same packed/SoA ownership. It must admit only a producer-proven complete, unchanged-shape render source with exact masks, binding/style/clip/effect metadata and accepted revision. Other edits, count/topology changes, decoration, missing field or stale publication fences must take the existing path. A failed setter must invalidate derived state before any accepted output; a later explicit operation rebuilds it. A failed/rejected publication must retain prior renderer-owned in-flight bytes and request the existing checkpoint/retry behavior. Split before publication must materialize an independently owned plan from the current prepared revision, never borrow a mutable pending buffer. This is the **proof obligation** for a possible wave, not a proposed implementation: no current source ledger identifies an expensive whole-path write/copy that can be removed without replacing it with publication traversal, alternate metadata or accessor work.

**Smallest next decisive fact:** obtain exact *timed-fixture* prepared source-row/semantic-row counts and branch attribution (new versus retained positioned rows, render SoA writes, revision comparisons) alongside per-set transaction setup and publication gather in one owned diagnostic, without a product patch or broad timing matrix. A hybrid becomes reviewable only if it names a source-to-consumer deletion that remains a deletion in the complete operation and can plausibly exceed the Node deficit under independent cold-output, prepared-query, split, rejection/retry, lifetime and seven-family gates. Unknown counts and inlined costs here must remain unknown until that diagnostic; another generic gather adapter or speculative cache is not warranted by this ledger.
